<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harzburg</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Harzburg was a royal castle built by Emperor Henry IV from about 1065 on a hill above present-day Bad Harzburg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> mountains. It was the chief of a ring of fortresses that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobility saw as instruments of royal oppression, and in 1074, during the Saxon revolt, rebels took and destroyed it, desecrating the royal graves in its church. Its ruins remain on the Burgberg.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The castle, also called the Große Harzburg, stood on the Großer Burgberg, 482.80 metres high, above the Radau valley. It is named Hartesburg in a deed of 1071, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>, Hart in Middle Low German. During Henry IV's minority the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> nobles had grown stronger, and the castle was raised between 1065 and 1068 in a programme of castle building across the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, sited by the king's architect, Bishop Benno II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, to protect the imperial palace of Goslar and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rammelsberg">Rammelsberg</a> mines. It held a large three-roomed great hall and a collegiate church, where Henry buried his brother, Duke Conrad II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and his son Henry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to the chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-hersfeld">Lambert of Hersfeld</a>, the Saxon Rebellion broke out on 29 June 1073, when nobles marched on the king at Goslar and he fled with the Imperial Regalia to the Harzburg. Besieged by forces under Otto of Nordheim and Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burchard-ii-of-halberstadt">Burchard II of Halberstadt</a>, he escaped to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> on the night of 10 August. Under the Treaty of Gerstungen of 1074 he agreed to slight his castles but pulled down only the walls and towers, and that spring peasants plundered the Harzburg and destroyed it. The outrage helped Henry defeat the rebels at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-langensalza-1075">Battle of Langensalza</a> on 9 June 1075.</p><p>Rebuilt under Emperor Frederick I during his conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a>, the castle was completed by Emperor Otto IV, who died there on 19 May 1218. It was pledged to the Counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wernigerode">Wernigerode</a> in 1269, occupied about 1370 by Duke Otto of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, and in the early 15th century became a robber baron's castle under the sons of Hans von Schwicheldt. It changed hands several times in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and demolition of what remained began in 1650.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Only fragments of the foundation walls, the towers and the well survive; one of the two bergfrieds has been partly rebuilt, and a modern bridge crosses the rock-cut moat. The 19-metre Canossa Column, raised in 1877 in honour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, stands on the plateau, which is reached by the Burgberg Cable Car.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Harzburg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1931 the Nazis joined monarchists, the German National People’s Party and others in the <b>Harzburg</b> Front against the Weimar Republic, an alliance the Nazis called tactical.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nazism">Nazism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
